{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 23, "model_name": "nvidia/llama-3.3-nemotron-super-49b-v1", "content": "Oregon's pioneering drug decriminalization policy will expire, giving way to a new law, HB4002, which reclassifies possession of small amounts of hard drugs as a crime. Those caught can opt for either a charge or treatment, involving a behavioral health program and a \"deflection program\" to avoid fines.\n\n***\n\nThe new law makes personal-use possession a misdemeanor, punishable by up to six months in jail. It aims to facilitate police crackdowns on public drug use and introduces stricter penalties for selling drugs near specific locations like parks. The law encourages, but does not require, counties to establish treatment alternatives.\n\n***\n\nCounties are left to develop programs based on their resources, which supporters see as beneficial but opponents argue may lead to inconsistent policies. Strict criteria must be met for \"deflection,\" including no other charges, warrants, or violent behavior, and being medically stable. If eligible, officers connect individuals with a mobile behavioral health outreach team, which must make contact within 30 minutes or the person will be taken to jail.\n\n***\n\nThe shift in policy reflects a complex approach to balancing punishment and treatment. As Oregon navigates this change, the impact on those struggling with addiction remains to be seen. \n\nP.P.S. As people of all age groups in this country struggle with addiction, it's clear that the issue affects how we eat, shop, and even use our tongue to express thoughts on the matter, often making a trip to the shopping mall a daunting task.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Oregon", "logprob": -0.07976089417934418}, {"token": "'s", "logprob": -0.3897145688533783}, {"token": " pioneering", "logprob": -0.14967069029808044}, {"token": " drug", "logprob": -0.01568657159805298